TITLE:: NdefRackKtlSim
summary:: a MIDI controller that is not plugged in, sending through Modality's own device
categories:: Libraries>NdefRack
related:: Classes/NdefRackKtl, Classes/MKtl, Classes/MIDIIn

DESCRIPTION::
A Modality link::Classes/MKtl:: made from its description, as for the real
controller, with a real MIDIMKtlDevice - listening to a MIDI source that is the
sim. The sim's knobs, faders and buttons send what the hardware sends into
link::Classes/MIDIIn::, where SuperCollider delivers MIDI arriving from a port;
from there on, every line that runs is the line the hardware runs: the device's
link::Classes/MIDIFunc::s, the element, its action and dependants, the rack.

code::
~sim = NdefRackKtlSim(\m1);        // MKtl(\m1), an Akai Midimix
~sim.gui;                          // its knobs, faders and buttons
~sim.move(\kn_1_1, 0.5);           // CC 16 at 64, as turning the knob does
~sim.press(\bt_1_1);               // note 1 on - a mute button down
~sim.release(\bt_1_1);             // note 1 off
~sim.free;
::

How: the sim adds a MIDI source of its own - a made-up uid, and an idInfo of
its own - to Modality's lookup, and makes the MKtl from it. A real Midimix
plugged in at the same time is therefore neither mistaken for the sim nor the
sim for it; but an MKtl name is one MKtl, so free the sim before making
code::MKtl(\m1):: for the hardware. Bindings of link::Classes/NdefRackKtl:: name
the MKtl, so what was learned on code::NdefRackKtlSim(\m1):: works on the real
code::MKtl(\m1)::.

The way back works too: the device's MIDIOut is the sim, so what the language
sends the controller - an element's code::value_::, an LED - lights the button
in the sim's window.

What it does not tell you: the controller's own timing and feel, and exactly
what it sends when a button is let go - the sim sends note off at velocity 127.


CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
The sim of that name, made the first time. Answers nil, with a warning, without
the Modality toolkit, for a description that is not MIDI, or when an MKtl of
that name exists.
ARGUMENT:: name
the MKtl's name.
ARGUMENT:: desc
the Modality description's file name: code::"akai-midimix":: unless given.

METHOD:: all
RETURNS:: the sims, by name.

METHOD:: uidBase
The first made-up uid; each sim takes the next.


INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: move
A knob or fader to a value, as the controller sends it.
ARGUMENT:: key
the element's name.
ARGUMENT:: value
0..1.

METHOD:: press
A button down: the on of a note pair, or a button of its own.
ARGUMENT:: key
the button's name - code::\bt_1_1:: for the pair code::bt_1_1_on::, code::bt_1_1_off::.
ARGUMENT:: value
0..1, the velocity; 1 unless given.

METHOD:: release
A button let go: the off of a note pair, or the button's own message at 0.
ARGUMENT:: key
the button's name.

METHOD:: gui
The controller in a window, laid out as its description places its elements.
Turning a knob there is code::move::; pressing a button is code::press:: and
letting go code::release::.

METHOD:: close
Close the window.

METHOD:: free
Close the window, free the MKtl and take the sim's source out of Modality's lookup.

METHOD:: controls
RETURNS:: what the window shows, code::(key:, element:, kind:, row:, column:):: each - kind code::\knob::, code::\slider:: or code::\button::.

METHOD:: isLit
ARGUMENT:: key
a button's name.
RETURNS:: whether the language has lit it.

METHOD:: name
RETURNS:: the MKtl's name.

METHOD:: descName
RETURNS:: the description's file name.

METHOD:: uid
RETURNS:: the made-up uid its messages come from.

METHOD:: mktl
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/MKtl::.

METHOD:: lookupName
RETURNS:: the name of its source in Modality's lookup.

METHOD:: leds
RETURNS:: what the language last sent, code::[chan, num] -> value::.

METHOD:: window
RETURNS:: the window, or nil.

METHOD:: views
RETURNS:: the window's views by control name: a link::Classes/Knob::, a link::Classes/Slider:: or a button's link::Classes/UserView::.

METHOD:: noteOn
What the device sends a note on with: lights the button.

METHOD:: noteOff
What the device sends a note off with.

METHOD:: control
What the device sends a control change with.

METHOD:: latency
0: the device sets it, and the sim sends nothing anywhere.
